A||N||3||FieroJoe||Good Fiero day today, except for broken bolts! *long*||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||07-05-2001||11:05 PM||||Today I got up early to install my new poly swaybar pivot busings and brand new end links, for starters.  I had a list of other things, but I decided that I better do this first.  So, get the car up on stands, wheels off, and start on the old crappy end links.<P>I manage to get those old rusty things off, and then I go for the pivot bushings.  Not budgeing with the rachet, so I decide I'll go slowly with the impact wrench.  Zipppp...snap!  There goes one bolt, head comes right off.  I was very careful with the other 3, but all the damn things broke off!<P>So I guess after 221,000 miles those things were pretty well rusted in.  I then spent the next 2 hours drilling out and retapping the holes, getting burning hot shavings in my face and all over my arms.  The bolts were so hard that I dulled 1 drill bit per hole!<P>So anyways, I get done drilling and tapping 3 of them, and then start on tapping out the 4th hole.  About halfway through it, the freaking tap breaks off inside of it!  I was pissed.  Luckily, my dad had a very good idea that saved me.<P>He took an old drill bit, ground the end flat, and then ground out a little fork into the top with the dremel.  <P>And it worked!  I put the little fork in there, and slowly and carefully backed out the broken piece of the tap.  The little tongs on the "fork" fit perfectly into the grooves on the tap.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7050011_copy.jpg"> <P>So next, after I get all the new bushings installed, which took me about 15 minutes total  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> to do, I decide that I'll paint my calipers, like I have been meaning to for a long time.  So spend a while, taping off, and then get them painted.  They came about pretty decent, at least for a job where I didnt clean them really really good and they were still mounted on the car.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7050009_copy_1.jpg"> <P>Next, I finished up wiring my radiator fan switch.  Since it has been getting pretty hot up here, and my engine running a little hotter than normal on some days, I decided to install a switch to turn on the radiator fan anytime I wanted.  I installed the switch under an ashtray, next to my ECM button (pushing button is same as putting paper clip in ALDL, but I didnt want to keep taking off the cover when I was getting a lot of codes everyday.)<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7050008_copy.jpg"> <P>I then changed my fuel filter, and got gasoline all over myself.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>After all that, I drove the car, and noticed an immediate difference in the handling from the new bushings.  I have a set of turns that I like to go around much faster than the posted speed that I drive almost everyday, and I could tell that the car held on a little better.  There also wasnt much drama coming from the tires when taking those corners.<P>I hope everybody liked my day as much as I did.  I got a lot done, and my car is looking/working better in time for the Michigan Fiero Club show next week!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000001||MooCow||07-06-2001||05:05 PM||||Well at least you got them in.  How much did they run you??  I like the idea of the ECM push button feature.<BR>Can't wait to finally meet you at the MI show next week.<P>                                Thanks Ken<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_7.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||10
Z||000002||DRH||07-06-2001||05:36 PM||||Wow, 3 great ideas there:<P>- the drill thing to remove a broken tap<BR>- a hard wired switch for codes<BR>- mounting switches in the ash tray slots (I don't smoke anymore anyway)<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000003||FieroJoe||07-07-2001||12:23 AM||||Ken - Yes, I can't wait for the show!  I'm getting very excited, less than one week almost!  The new endlinks with poly bushings were $16.95, and the new poly pivot bushings/brackets were also $16.95 from the Fiero Store.<P>DRH - I was pretty impressed that our homemade tap extractor work so well too!  The first one we made had the little forks break off, so I just reground it flat and made two more grooves.  I tried to grind it really fast, and it got very hot.  Maybe this time they didnt break because it got "heat treated"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The button really isnt very "hard wired".  All I did was strip the ends of the wires and shove them into the ALDL connector  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Under or in the ashtrays is about the only place I have left to mount switches before they start to show.  Currently, I have 7 switches/buttons hidden in my car for my various electrical gizmos and mods.  I love all my little electronics, but I dont want any to show.<P>Thanks for the comments!||||reg||1
